    card title "A jump at sixty-five"

Patients rushed into hospital just after their sixty-fifth birthday are less likely to d (@draw ticks@)ie that week than patients rushed in just before. Is that Medicare, and how  (@point at.data [65,2.75] gesture tap@)hard would we have to push before the claim falls over?
    draw axes

The claim rests on one line: at sixty-five almost every Amer (@draw cut_lab@)ican becomes eligible for Medicare, the public insurance for older people.
    draw cut

These dots are made up, but close in size to the real study: each is the share of (@point at.ref l9 gesture circle@) emergency patients of one age who died within a week.
    draw dots_l madeup

The risk of death climbs with age, so the dots rise, but right at sixty-five they step down.
    draw dots_r

Nothing else about a person's health should jump on that birthday. So a step exactly at the line is credited to the thing that switched there, and that is the whole logic of regression discontinuity.
    highlight cut

The quick way to measure the step: fit a straight line on e (@draw fit_r@)ach side, five years out, and read the gap where they reach the birthday.
    draw fit_l

That gap is minus one and a half percentage points, with a 95 percent margin of four tenths.
    draw jump_arrow readout m95

    quiz "Fit the same lines over just two years either side instead of five. What happens to the gap?" judge false
        intro: Before we change anything, a guess.
        right: It shrinks, to about one point, because the far-away dots were tilting the lines.
        * It shrinks
        * It grows
        * It stays the same

Here are the two-year lines.
    animate h 2 duration 3

The gap is now one point. Same dots, a different answer.
    highlight readout effect underline color #e0a830

Which one should we believe?
    point at.ref l0 gesture circle

The answer lies in the curve the dots follow: fa (@draw true_r@)r from sixty-five it bends, and a straight line stretched ove (@highlight fit_r@)r a bend tilts to chase it.
    draw true_l

    keep fit_l fit_r

Stretch the lines back out to five years, and watch each one swing away from the curve right where it meets the birthday.
    animate h 5 duration 3

    erase fit_l_ghost fit_r_ghost

So why not always look through a narrow window?
    animate h 1 duration 3

At one year the gap is still about one point, but it rests on just two dots a side, and its 95 percent margin swells to almost nine tenths.
    highlight readout effect underline color #e0a830

That is the trade every discontinuity makes: a wide window buys precision and risks bias, a narrow one cuts the bias and pays in noise.
    highlight fit_l fit_r

And the window is only the first test (@erase m95 madeup@). A discontinuity also breaks if people could  (@draw map2@)choose their side of the line, or if crossing it changed (@draw map3@) the treatment for some people and not others.
    draw map1

We push on each in turn, and see whether this drop at sixty-five survives.
    highlight jump_arrow

