    card title "How wide a window?"

How wide a window should a discontinuity look through, when every width gives a different answer?
    draw t_frame dots_l dots_r

Here are  (@draw cut cut_lab@)the made-up death r (@draw top_fit@)ates around sixty-five again, one dot for each half year of age.

Lines fitted two years eit (@highlight edge_l edge_r@)her side, between the grey edges, put t (@highlight readout effect underline color #e0a830@)he drop at one point.
    draw readout m95

Below them goes a second chart: across, t (@draw b_xt b_h1 b_h5@)he width of the window in years each side; up, (@draw b_title@) the gap that window reports.
    draw b_xax b_yax zero zero_lab

Trace the gap at every width and you g (@draw handle@)et this curve: flat for narrow windows, then sliding down (@point at.data [69.5,13.36] gesture tap duration 2@) as the window reaches the bend.
    draw est_curve

Because we made these numbers up, we know the true drop: exactly one point, the dashed line.
    draw truth truth_lab

Around the curve goes its 95 percent margin, wide when the window holds few people, and narrowing as it takes in more.
    draw ci_g

At five years the band is narrow and lies wholly below the truth: precise, and wrong.
    point at.data [69.6,13] gesture circle duration 5

At one year it covers the truth, but it is so wide that it almost reaches zero.
    point at.data [62.3,22] gesture circle duration 5

Your turn: grab the violet dot on the lower curve and move it left and right, and watch the lines above tilt as the window grows.
    explore params ["h"] watch "Watch the window sweep from half a year to five: the lines above tilt, and the gap below slides away from the truth."

What you should have seen: from one to two years the gap barely moves while the band tightens; past three, it runs away from the truth.
    animate h 1.5 duration 4

Real researchers cannot see a truth line, so they choose the width by rule. The rule Imbens and Kalyanaraman proposed in 20 (@draw rule_tick@)12 balances bias against noise, and in our made-up data it lands near three years.
    animate h 3 duration 3

At that width the bias, about a tenth of a point, is not small next to a standard error of about a quarter, so the usual margin comes out too narrow.
    highlight ci_band

Calonico, Cattaneo and Titiunik showed in 2014 how to correct for that bias and wide (@draw rb_lab@)n the margin to match, like these darker lines, and that is now the standard report.
    draw rb_hi rb_lo

The real study behind our made-up dots is Card, Dobkin and Maestas, 2009: about  (@draw tb_top@)four hundred thousand emergency patients aged sixty to seventy, admitted to California hospitals between 1992 and 2002.
    erase b_frame est_curve handle truth truth_lab ci_g rb_hi rb_lo rb_lab rule_tick m95 t_frame cut cut_lab dots_l dots_r top_fit readout

With a smooth curve on each side across the whole decade, and with (@draw tr0_b@) controls for the patients, deaths within a week fell  (@draw tr0_c@)by one point at sixty-five, with a standard error of tw (@draw tb_base@)o tenths, from a base of about five percent.
    draw tr0_a

A straight line fitted close to the birthday, with a rul (@draw tr1_b tr1_c@)e-of-thumb window, gave minus point eight, also with a standard error of two tenths.
    draw tr1_a

And a more flexible curve, a cubic, gave minu (@draw tr2_b tr2_c@)s point seven, with a standard error of three tenths: the drop shrin (@highlight tr0_b tr1_b tr2_b effect box color #e0a830@)ks a little, but it never disappears.
    draw tr2_a

    quiz "A scholarship goes to pupils above a test-score cutoff. Within 5 marks of it the gain in later grades is 4 points, within 10 marks also 4, and within 30 marks it is 9. Which belongs in the headline?" intro "A new case."
        right: The 4, because it holds steady as the window changes; the jump to 9 at wide windows is the bend, shown beside it rather than hidden.
        wrong: More pupils only help if a straight line still fits that far from the cutoff.
        + 4: it holds in the narrow windows
        * 9: it uses the most pupils
        * Neither: the design has failed

So a discontinuity estimate is not one number but a curve, and the credible report shows that curve, not its most flattering point.
    highlight tb_head effect underline color #e0a830

## Who chose their side?
