    text chk1 "Count: smooth" at.data [61.7,118.6] cites ["mccrary2008","cdm2009"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text chk2 "Illness, risk: smooth" at.data [62.1,115.2] cites ["cdm2009"] font_size 32 hidden true
    group chart members ["axes","cut","cut_lab","p_l","p_r","e_l","e_r","p_name","p_jump","p_arrow","e_name","e_jump","chk1","chk2","src"] hidden true
    scratch dsp work ["Almond et al. 2010: fewer deaths under 1,500 g","Barreca et al. 2011: heaped babies sicker, effect shrinks","Reply 2011: smaller, still large","Would settle it: unrounded weights"] x 500 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Who chose their side?"

A window can be chosen with care and a discontinuity can still fa (@draw ticks@)il, if people could decide which side of the line they landed on.
    draw axes

Nobody chooses a birthday, so age looks safe. (@draw cut_lab@) But a hospital study counts patients, not birthdays, and a  (@highlight cut@)patient can sometimes choose when to come in.
    draw cut

Take planned stays, a new hip or a gall bladder operation. Here is the number of patien (@draw p_name@)ts at each age, set to one hundred just before sixty-five.
    draw p_l

Just after the birthday the count jumps by about twelve percent, (@draw p_arrow p_jump@) with a standard error of half a point: the shape here is drawn by hand, but that jump is the rea (@highlight p_jump effect underline color #e0a830@)l study's, from the same California hospital records.
    draw p_r

Why would twelve percent more people need a new hip the month after turning sixty-five?
    point at.ref p_arrow gesture circle duration 4

They don't. People with thin coverage put the operation off, and book it once Medicare pays.
    highlight p_r

So the patients just over sixty-five are no longer like those just under: the over side has gained people who would not have been in hospital a month earlier.
    highlight p_l

Compare death rates across that line, and the effect of Medicare is mixed up with a change in who is being counted.
    point at.data [65,106] gesture underline duration 6

That is why the first test of a discontinuity ignores the outcome and simply counts: does the number of people jump at the line? Justin McCrary turned that count into a formal test in 2008.
    highlight p_jump effect box color #e0a830

Card, Dobkin and Maestas met (@erase ticks@) the problem head on. They kept only emergenci (@draw e_l@)es that cannot wait, like heart attacks and strokes, the kind tha (@draw e_name@)t arrive as often on a Sunday as on a Tuesday.
    fade p_l p_r p_arrow p_jump p_name to 0.25

For those, the count runs straight through sixty-five: a rise of (@draw e_jump src@) six tenths of a percent, with a standard error of nine tenths.
    draw e_r

A smooth count is needed, but it is not enough, so (@highlight e_l e_r@) they also checked who the patients were.
    draw chk1

Their other illnesses, and their risk of death predicted from age, sex, race (@point at.data [65,100.3] gesture circle duration 3@) and diagnosis, showed no jump at the line either.
    draw chk2

So age passes, but only in the right sample. A reported income is like a booked operation: the person who  (@highlight p_arrow@)gains from the line also controls the number.
    highlight p_r

    quiz "Babies born under 1,500 grams get extra care. A count of births shows a spike at exactly 1,500. What is the likeliest reason?" intro "A new case."
        right: Weights recorded as round numbers pile up at 1,500, so the babies in that spike may not be like the babies around them.
        wrong: A count can jump without anyone choosing a side: think about how a weight gets written down.
        * Doctors push babies across the line
        * Nothing: a weight cannot be chosen
        + Weights written as round numbers pile up

This is a live case. Almond, Doyle, Kowalski and Wil (@draw dsp_box dsp_line_1@)liams found in 2010 that babies just under 1,500 grams, who get extra care, w (@highlight dsp_line_1 effect underline color #e0a830@)ere less likely to die in their first year.
    fade chart to 0.08

Barreca and colleagues answered in 2011 that babies at round weights come disproportionately from poorer, less (@highlight dsp_line_2 effect underline color #e0a830@) healthy births, and that setting them aside shrinks that benefit sharply.
    draw dsp_line_2

The original authors replied that the benefit shrinks but stays large (@highlight dsp_line_3 effect underline color #e0a830@), above all in hospitals with weaker units for newborns.
    draw dsp_line_3

What would settle it is weights recorded without  (@highlight dsp_line_4 effect underline color #e0a830@)rounding, so that no babies pile up at the line.
    draw dsp_line_4

So the second test asks who could re (@fade chart to 1@)ach the l (@fade p_l p_r p_arrow p_jump p_name to 0.25@)ine. A birthday cannot be moved, a booking or a reported incom (@highlight e_r e_l@)e can, and a count at the line is the first way to tell which you have.
    erase dsp

## Who did the line move?
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: cdm2009
      title: Does Medicare Save Lives?
      authors: Card, Dobkin and Maestas, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2009
      finding: 7-day deaths fall 1.0 point (SE 0.2) at 65
    - id: htv2001
      title: Identification and Estimation of Treatment Effects with a Regression-Discontinuity Design
      authors: Hahn, Todd and van der Klaauw, Econometrica
      year: 2001
      finding: Fuzzy RD is IV at the cutoff
takeaways:
    - Show the estimate at every width
    - Count the people at the line first
    - Divide only if one road leads on

    box nz "Turns 65" thick x 160 y 560 width 290 height 120 font_size 50 style.stroke #4f8a4b hidden true
    box nd "Insured" thick x 560 y 560 width 250 height 120 font_size 50 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box ny "Dies in a week" thick x 930 y 560 width 345 height 120 font_size 50 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    math sz tex Z_i size 54 colors.Z_i #4f8a4b x 160 y 455 hidden true
    math sd tex D_i size 54 colors.D_i #2f6b8f x 560 y 455 hidden true
    math sy tex Y_i size 54 x 930 y 455 hidden true
    arrow a_zd thick #3d3833 from.x 308 from.y 560 to.x 432 to.y 560 hidden true
    arrow a_dy thick #3d3833 from.x 688 from.y 560 to.x 754 to.y 560 hidden true
    text l_zd "+8 pts (SE 0.2)" #2f6b8f cites ["cdm2009"] x 380 y 665 font_size 40 hidden true
    path a_zy thick #8a5fa8 points [[160,621],[160,700],[930,700],[930,624]] hidden true
    arrow a_zy_tip thick #8a5fa8 from.x 930 from.y 680 to.x 930 to.y 622 hidden true
    text l_zy "−1.0 pts (SE 0.2)" #8a5fa8 cites ["cdm2009"] x 760 y 740 font_size 40 hidden true
    text src "Card, Dobkin, Maestas 2009" #8f887c cites ["cdm2009"] x 280 y 742 font_size 36 hidden true
    group rf members ["a_zy","a_zy_tip","l_zy"] hidden true
    box nb "Better cover" thick x 560 y 320 width 330 height 120 font_size 50 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    arrow a_zb thick #3d3833 from.x 250 from.y 494 to.x 408 to.y 345 hidden true
    arrow a_by thick #3d3833 from.x 712 from.y 345 to.x 850 to.y 494 hidden true
    text l_zb "Medicare first: +47 (SE 0.4)" cites ["cdm2009"] x 590 y 232 font_size 36 hidden true
    group road2 members ["nb","a_zb","a_by","l_zb"] hidden true
    math ratio tex "\\hat\\tau = \\frac{{\\Delta Y}}{{\\Delta D}}" size 54 colors {"{\\Delta Y}":"#8a5fa8","{\\Delta D}":"#2f6b8f","\\hat\\tau":"#8a5fa8"} x 165 y 200 hidden true
    mark ratio_x around ratio kind cross hidden true
    scratch bound work ["Uninsured, just under 65:","5 % died in a week",{"tex":"0.08 \\times 5 = 0.4"},"at most 40 % of the drop"] x 760 y 228 font_size 29 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["nz","nd","ny","sz","sd","sy","a_zd","a_dy","l_zd","a_zy","a_zy_tip","l_zy","src","rf","nb","a_zb","a_by","l_zb","road2","ratio","ratio_x"] hidden true

    card title "Who did the line move?"

Turning sixty-five does not insure everyone who lacked cover, and it changes the insurance of many who a (@draw sz@)lready had some. So what exactly does the drop at sixty-five measure?
    draw nz

Call crossing the line Z, and having any insurance at a (@draw sd@)ll D: both are switches, zero or one, for each patient.
    draw a_zd nd

Among the emergency patients, crossing sixty-five c (@highlight nz effect box color #e0a830@)ut the share with no insurance from about ten percent to two. So D jump (@highlight l_zd effect underline color #e0a830@)s by eight points, with a standard error of two tenths: most were covered already.
    draw l_zd src

The outcome Y is death within a week, and if coverage is wha (@draw sy@)t saves lives, the line works through D.
    draw a_dy ny

And Y fell by one point at the line, with a standard error of two tenths: the effect of crossing itself, diluted, since (@highlight l_zd effect underline color #e0a830@) only eight in a hundred had their coverage change.
    draw rf

If D is the only road, the fuzzy design undoes that dilution: it divides the jump in Y (@highlight ratio part "{\\Delta Y}" effect underline color #e0a830@) by the jump in D, the same  (@highlight ratio part "{\\Delta D}" effect underline color #e0a830@)ratio as an instrumental variable, with crossing the line as the instrument.
    draw ratio

If coverage were the only road, that is minus one over eight hundredths: twelve and a half poin (@highlight ratio effect underline color #e0a830@)ts fewer deaths among the newly insured.
    morph ratio tex "\\hat\\tau = \\frac{-1.0}{0.08} = -12.5"

Check that against the patients themselves. Uninsured patients just under sixty-f (@draw bound_line_2@)ive died within a week about five percent of the time.
    draw bound_box bound_line_1

Coverage cannot push a risk below zero, so eight in a hundred, each spared at most five percent, a (@draw bound_line_4@)ccount for at most four tenths of a point.
    draw bound_line_3

The rest must travel an (@draw nb a_zb a_by@)other road. At sixty-five nearly half of these patients switch  (@draw l_zb@)to Medicare as their main insurer, often from plans that limited car (@highlight nb effect box color #e0a830@)e, and they get more done in hospital.
    erase bound

That road bypasses D, so dividing by the jump in D hands all of the (@highlight a_zb a_by@) drop to coverage alone, and the ratio comes out impossibly large.
    draw ratio_x

A fuzzy ratio is honest only when crossing the (@highlight a_zd a_dy@) line moves the outcome through the treatment alone, and when  (@highlight a_zb a_by@)no one is pushed out of treatment by crossing.
    erase ratio ratio_x

When both hold, the ratio is the effect for the compliers at the cutoff: the people whose coverage changed because they turned sixty-five, and nobody else.
    highlight nd effect box color #e0a830

Card, Dobkin and Maestas did not divide. They reported the drop as the effect of reaching Medicare age: more coverage for a few, and better coverage for many.
    highlight l_zy effect box color #e0a830

    quiz "A clinic offers a free follow-up visit to patients with a risk score above 50. At 50 the share who get a visit jumps from 10 to 50 percent, and readmissions fall by 2 points. What does the ratio estimate?" intro "A new case."
        right: Two points over forty points of take-up is five, and it speaks only for patients who got the visit because they crossed 50, if the score does nothing else at 50.
        wrong: Divide by how much the cutoff moved the visits, then ask whose visits it moved.
        * A 2-point fall for all above 50
        + A 5-point fall for those the cutoff sent
        * A 5-point fall for all who had a visit

A discontinuity is believable  (@draw takeaway_1@)wh (@move takeaway_1 to.x 500 to.y 300 duration 0.1@)en its answer holds as the window cha (@highlight l_zy effect underline color #e0a830@)nges, not at one chosen width.
    move fig scale 0.62 to.x 500 to.y 485 duration 1.5

It (@move takeaway_2 to.x 500 to.y 236 duration 0.1@) needs a line nobody could choose their side of, a (@highlight nz effect box color #e0a830@)nd a count of people at the line is the first check.
    draw takeaway_2

And (@move takeaway_3 to.x 500 to.y 172 duration 0.1@) a fuzzy ratio needs every road from the line t (@highlight nb effect box color #e0a830@)o pass through the treatment; then it speaks for t (@highlight nd effect box color #e0a830@)he people the line moved.
    draw takeaway_3

So does Medicare save lives? For emergency patients at sixty-five, the drop survives the window and the count.
    highlight a_zy a_zy_tip l_zy effect box color #e0a830

And the third test says what it is: the effect of reaching Medicare, better cover as much as any cover, not of being insured alone.
    highlight nb nd effect box color #e0a830

## Where next
